2. Reference Topology

Internal hosts → IOS XE NAT router → External network

Role 	Address
Client A 	10.1.1.10
Client B 	10.1.1.11
Internal server 	10.1.1.20
Router LAN interface 	10.1.1.1
Router outside interface 	203.0.113.10
External server 	198.51.100.20
External client 	198.51.100.30

The external addresses are documentation addresses.

Packet notation

source-IP:source-port → destination-IP:destination-port

Example:

10.1.1.10:50000 → 198.51.100.20:443


3. Cisco NAT Terminology

Term 	Definition 	Example
Inside local 	Inside host’s address as represented inside 	10.1.1.10
Inside global 	Inside host’s address as represented outside 	203.0.113.10
Outside global 	Outside host’s address in its outside network 	198.51.100.20
Outside local 	Outside host’s address as represented inside 	Usually 198.51.100.20, unless translated

Basic inside-source translation

Outbound:

10.1.1.10 → 198.51.100.20

becomes:

203.0.113.10 → 198.51.100.20

Return:

198.51.100.20 → 203.0.113.10

becomes:

198.51.100.20 → 10.1.1.10

Important distinction: Inside-source NAT changes the source address outbound and the destination address on the reverse path.


4. NAT Packet Processing

4.1 Classic inside-to-outside flow

    A packet arrives on an interface designated as NAT inside.
    Routing determines the forwarding path.
    NAT selects an applicable rule or existing translation.
    The source address—and possibly source port—is translated.
    Relevant checksums are updated.
    The router forwards the packet outside.


4.2 Classic outside-to-inside return flow

    The reply arrives on a NAT outside interface.
    NAT identifies the reverse translation.
    The destination is translated to the inside-local endpoint.
    Routing forwards the packet internally.


This is a simplified description. NVI, NAT64, and interactions with other services can use different processing details.

4.3 Reachability requirements

NAT still requires:

    A route toward the destination.
    Upstream reachability to translated addresses.
    A valid internal return route.
    Appropriate access policies.
    A return path through the device holding the translation, or a supported state-sharing design.



5. Static Inside-Source NAT

Function

Creates a permanent one-to-one mapping:

10.1.1.10 ↔ 203.0.113.10

Packet flow

Stage 	Packet
Client sends 	10.1.1.10:50000 → 198.51.100.20:443
Router translates source 	203.0.113.10:50000 → 198.51.100.20:443
External server replies 	198.51.100.20:443 → 203.0.113.10:50000
Router translates destination 	198.51.100.20:443 → 10.1.1.10:50000

Characteristics

    The address mapping is configured permanently.
    Basic address-only static NAT preserves ports.
    Externally initiated connections can use the mapping if routing and access policies permit.
    A dedicated translated address is associated with the inside endpoint.


Illustrative command

ip nat inside source static 10.1.1.10 203.0.113.10

Interface NAT roles and routing must also be configured.


6. Dynamic Inside-Source NAT

Function

Allocates a translated address from a pool when eligible traffic arrives.

Example pool:

203.0.113.10–203.0.113.12

Packet flow

    Client A sends a packet from 10.1.1.10.
    No applicable dynamic mapping exists.
    NAT allocates 203.0.113.11.
    The packet source becomes 203.0.113.11.
    Replies are translated back to 10.1.1.10.
    The mapping eventually expires.


Characteristics

    Addresses are allocated dynamically.
    Pure dynamic NAT does not share an address among many inside hosts through PAT.
    Pool exhaustion prevents additional address allocations.
    An unsolicited inbound packet generally cannot create the required dynamic inside mapping.


Typical configuration elements

    An ACL identifying eligible traffic.
    ip nat pool …
    ip nat inside source list … pool …


Distinction: Dynamic NAT allocates addresses; PAT multiplexes flows.


7. PAT / NAT Overload

Function

Allows multiple inside hosts to share one or more translated addresses.

Packet flow

Host 	Before PAT 	After PAT
Client A 	10.1.1.10:50000 → 198.51.100.20:443 	203.0.113.10:50000 → 198.51.100.20:443
Client B 	10.1.1.11:50000 → 198.51.100.20:443 	203.0.113.10:50001 → 198.51.100.20:443

Return traffic to translated port 50000 reaches Client A. Return traffic to 50001 reaches Client B.

Variants

Interface overload

Uses an interface address:

ip nat inside source list NAT-LAN interface GigabitEthernet0/0/0 overload

Pool overload

Uses addresses from a pool:

ip nat inside source list NAT-LAN pool NAT-POOL overload

Resource considerations

PAT capacity depends on:

    Available translated addresses and ports.
    Protocol and mapping behavior.
    Active flows.
    Translation limits.
    Platform memory and dataplane capacity.


It is inaccurate to describe PAT capacity simply as “65,535 users per public address.”


8. Static PAT / Port Forwarding

Function

Maps a particular external service endpoint to an internal endpoint.

Mapping:

TCP 203.0.113.10:8443 → 10.1.1.20:443

Packet flow

Stage 	Packet
External request 	198.51.100.30:51000 → 203.0.113.10:8443
Delivered internally 	198.51.100.30:51000 → 10.1.1.20:443
Server reply 	10.1.1.20:443 → 198.51.100.30:51000
Translated reply 	203.0.113.10:8443 → 198.51.100.30:51000

Illustrative command

ip nat inside source static tcp 10.1.1.20 443 203.0.113.10 8443

Considerations

    TCP and UDP require separate mappings.
    Several services can share a public address using different ports.
    Check for conflicts with router-local services.
    Port forwarding does not automatically permit traffic through an ACL or firewall.



9. NAT Pools and Static Mapping Options

9.1 Address pools

A NAT pool defines eligible translated addresses.

Flow: New packet → allocate an address or address/port combination → create mapping → forward.

Upstream devices must route or otherwise deliver traffic for those addresses to the NAT router.

9.2 Additional options

Depending on platform and release, IOS XE can provide options for:

    Static network or prefix mappings.
    Interface-based static PAT.
    Route-map-associated static mappings.
    extendable static translations.
    Alias/ARP behavior.
    Automatic route creation in applicable outside-source configurations.


These options have different purposes.

For example, extendable permits certain static translation combinations to coexist; it does not provide unrestricted support for every overlapping policy design.


10. ACL-Based NAT Selection and Translation Exclusion

Function

An ACL identifies traffic eligible for a NAT rule.

Packet examples

External traffic:

10.1.1.10 → 198.51.100.20

→ matches NAT selection

→ source is translated.

Internal-to-internal traffic:

10.1.1.10 → 10.2.2.20

→ excluded from this NAT rule

→ addresses remain unchanged unless another rule applies.

Important distinction

A deny entry in a NAT-selection ACL does not itself drop the packet.

The ACL is selecting translation eligibility, not necessarily filtering forwarding.

IOS XE terminology

“NAT exemption” is commonly implemented by excluding traffic from applicable NAT selection. ASA identity-NAT syntax should not be assumed to exist on IOS XE.


11. Route-Map and Policy NAT

Function

Chooses a translation using supported policy criteria, such as source, destination, and—in applicable designs—egress-related conditions.

Example

Destination 	Source translation
Partner network 	10.1.1.10 → 172.20.1.10
Other external networks 	10.1.1.10 → 203.0.113.10

Packet flow

    Packet arrives.
    Routing and supported policy matching identify the applicable rule.
    NAT selects the associated static mapping or pool.
    Return traffic reverses the selected translation.


Use cases

    Partner-specific address presentation.
    Different pools for different destinations.
    Multi-ISP translation policies.
    Conditional static translation, where supported.


Important: A NAT route map does not replace routing or independently provide ISP failover.


12. Outside-Source NAT

Function

Changes how an outside endpoint’s address is represented inside.

Mapping:

    Outside global: 198.51.100.20
    Outside local: 172.16.100.20


Outside-to-inside flow

Before translation:

198.51.100.20:443 → 10.1.1.10:50000

After translation:

172.16.100.20:443 → 10.1.1.10:50000

Inside-to-outside flow

Before translation:

10.1.1.10:50000 → 172.16.100.20:443

After translation:

10.1.1.10:50000 → 198.51.100.20:443

Use cases

    Representing a remote network through an alias range.
    Resolving address overlap.


Routing consideration

The inside host must reach the outside-local alias through the NAT router. Some applicable command forms offer add-route; verify its exact behavior for the release.


13. Overlapping Networks and Both-Endpoint Translation

Scenario

Both sites use 10.1.1.0/24.

Endpoint 	Actual address 	Address presented remotely
Site A client 	10.1.1.10 	172.20.1.10
Site B server 	10.1.1.20 	172.21.1.20

Forward flow

Site A sends:

10.1.1.10:50000 → 172.21.1.20:443

After both translations:

172.20.1.10:50000 → 10.1.1.20:443

Return flow

Site B replies:

10.1.1.20:443 → 172.20.1.10:50000

After reverse translation:

172.21.1.20:443 → 10.1.1.10:50000

IOS XE considerations

Supported combinations of inside-source, outside-source, and policy mechanisms can translate both endpoints.

Do not assume ASA “twice NAT” configuration maps directly to IOS XE.

Also distinguish this from double NAT, which can mean translation at two separate routers.


14. VRF-Aware NAT

Function

Associates translation with routing contexts, allowing deployments with overlapping tenant addresses.

Example

VRF 	Original endpoint 	Translated endpoint
VRF-A 	10.1.1.10:50000 	203.0.113.10:50000
VRF-B 	10.1.1.10:50000 	203.0.113.11:50000

Packet flow

    The packet arrives in a particular VRF.
    NAT selects a translation applicable to that context.
    Routing forwards the packet.
    Return processing identifies the correct mapping and inside context.


Design questions

    Is translation within one VRF?
    Is it between a VRF and the global table?
    Is the intended inter-VRF design supported?
    Where are the external and internal routes?
    Is a command option such as match-in-vrf applicable?


NAT does not automatically perform the route leaking required by every VRF design.


15. NAT with Multiple ISPs

Function

Uses translated addresses appropriate to the chosen external path.

Packet flow

Through ISP-A:

10.1.1.10 → NAT using ISP-A address → destination

Through ISP-B:

10.1.1.10 → NAT using ISP-B address → destination

Required coordination

    Routing or policy-based routing.
    NAT rule selection.
    ISP-specific address reachability.
    Link tracking.
    Return-path consistency.
    Existing translation behavior during failover.


Session limitation

If a connection changes from ISP-A’s public address to ISP-B’s address, the remote endpoint generally sees a different flow.

Routing failover alone does not preserve NAT sessions.


16. Hairpin NAT, NVI, and NAT-on-a-Stick

16.1 Hairpin NAT

An internal client accesses an internal server through its public address.

Stage 	Packet
Client request 	10.1.1.10:50000 → 203.0.113.10:443
Delivered to server 	10.1.1.1:60000 → 10.1.1.20:443
Server reply 	10.1.1.20:443 → 10.1.1.1:60000
Delivered to client 	203.0.113.10:443 → 10.1.1.10:50000

Source translation in this example forces the reply through the NAT device. Without it, a same-subnet server might reply directly to the client.

Support caution: Classic NAT does not universally support every same-interface hairpin design.

16.2 NAT Virtual Interface

Where supported, NVI uses NAT-enabled interfaces instead of traditional inside/outside roles.

Conceptually:

Arrival → routing/NAT processing → translation → forwarding

Availability and restrictions vary by platform and release.

16.3 NAT-on-a-stick

This is a topology where traffic enters and exits through the same physical interface, potentially through separate subinterfaces or logical paths.

NVI and NAT-on-a-stick are different concepts. Neither automatically guarantees every hairpin scenario.


17. Stateful NAT64

Function

Allows IPv6 clients to communicate with IPv4 servers.

Role 	Address
IPv6 client 	2001:db8:1::10
IPv4 server 	198.51.100.20
IPv6 representation of server 	64:ff9b::c633:6414
NAT64 IPv4 address 	203.0.113.10

Forward flow

IPv6 packet:

[2001:db8:1::10]:50000 → [64:ff9b::c633:6414]:443

Translated IPv4 packet:

203.0.113.10:60000 → 198.51.100.20:443

Return flow

IPv4 reply:

198.51.100.20:443 → 203.0.113.10:60000

Translated IPv6 reply:

[64:ff9b::c633:6414]:443 → [2001:db8:1::10]:50000

Processing requirements

NAT64 converts:

    IP headers and address family.
    Addresses and potentially ports.
    Relevant checksums.
    Applicable ICMP messages.


Verify platform-specific protocol, VRF, ALG, fragmentation, redundancy, and scale restrictions.


18. Related IPv6 Translation Technologies

Technology 	Purpose 	Support consideration
DNS64 	Synthesizes IPv6 DNS answers representing IPv4 destinations 	DNS function, not NAT itself
Stateless IPv4/IPv6 translation 	Uses algorithmic mappings without per-flow translation state 	Verify explicit implementation support
NPTv6 	Stateless IPv6 prefix translation 	Do not assume universal IOS XE support
464XLAT 	Combines CLAT and PLAT translation 	Verify which component is supported

464XLAT flow

IPv4 application → CLAT → IPv6-only network → PLAT/NAT64 → IPv4 server

NAT-PT is an obsolete historical mechanism and should not be treated as modern NAT64.


19. Application Layer Gateways

Function

An ALG handles supported protocols containing addresses or ports inside application payloads.

Packet flow

    NAT translates the IP/transport header.
    The ALG examines supported signaling.
    Embedded endpoint information may be rewritten.
    Related translation entries may be created.


Examples requiring release verification

    FTP.
    SIP.
    H.323.
    DNS-related translation behavior.
    Other documented protocol helpers.


FTP example

The control connection works, but the application advertises an endpoint for a separate data connection. The ALG may rewrite that endpoint and establish related translation state.

SIP example

SIP signaling can contain addresses in headers or SDP. An ALG may rewrite supported fields so subsequent media flows use reachable endpoints.

Limitations

    Encrypted payloads generally cannot be rewritten this way.
    Nonstandard ports can require specific handling.
    Application-native NAT traversal can interact with ALG behavior.
    ALG state and ordinary NAT state may have different operational requirements.



20. ICMP, Fragments, and Checksums

ICMP echo

NAT can translate ICMP identifiers to distinguish requests sharing a translated address.

ICMP errors

An ICMP error can quote the original packet. Translation may require changes to:

    Outer addresses.
    Quoted addresses.
    Quoted transport information.
    Checksums.


Fragmentation

The first fragment normally carries transport-header information; later fragments do not.

The router must use supported fragment association or reassembly mechanisms to translate consistently.

Areas to validate

    Out-of-order fragments.
    Fragment resource limits.
    Path MTU discovery.
    NAT64 header-size differences.
    Unsupported protocol or fragmentation cases.



21. Translation Lifecycle, Timeouts, and Limits

Lifecycle

First packet → mapping creation → reuse → expiration or removal

Static address mappings can remain configured while associated per-flow entries have separate lifecycles.

Dynamic address mappings and child flow entries can also age differently.

Timeout controls

Depending on release, controls can exist for:

    General translation aging.
    TCP, UDP, and ICMP.
    TCP establishment and closing states.
    DNS or other protocol-specific state.


Resource limits

Potential controls include:

    Maximum total translations.
    Per-host or other scoped limits.
    Address-pool capacity.
    Port resources.
    Dataplane capacity.


Exhaustion flow

New packet → allocation requested → address, port, or translation limit unavailable → allocation fails.

Very short timers can interrupt valid quiet sessions. Very long timers retain resources unnecessarily.


22. CGN-Related Features and Hardware Forwarding

Carrier-grade NAT flow

Client:

192.168.1.10:50000

After home-router PAT:

100.64.1.10:51000

After provider NAT:

203.0.113.10:62000

100.64.0.0/10 is shared address space commonly used in this context.

Specialized features to verify

On applicable platforms and releases:

    High-scale translation processing.
    Port-block or bulk port allocation.
    Subscriber-oriented limits.
    Translation-event export.
    Mapping and filtering behavior.
    Logging reduction mechanisms.


These are not universal branch-router capabilities.

Hardware performance

NAT performance depends on:

    Packet rate and size.
    New-session creation rate.
    Active translation count.
    ALGs and fragments.
    Other enabled services.
    Platform throughput constraints.


Ordinary PAT support does not establish suitability for CGN-scale workloads.


23. NAT High Availability

Stateless failover

A second router takes over forwarding, but dynamic translation state may be absent. Existing connections can fail.

Stateful redundancy

Where supported:

    Active router creates a translation.
    Supported state is synchronized.
    Peer takes over after failure.
    Existing flows may continue if network reachability and required state are preserved.


Validate

    Supported redundancy architecture.
    Router and release compatibility.
    Translation and ALG state synchronization.
    Interface/address takeover.
    Routing convergence.
    VRF and NAT64 restrictions.


HSRP alone does not synchronize NAT translations.


24. NAT in EVPN/VXLAN Fabrics

Typical service placement

Client → access leaf → fabric → border/services router → NAT → external network

Before the NAT service point:

10.1.1.10:50000 → 198.51.100.20:443

After NAT:

203.0.113.10:50000 → 198.51.100.20:443

Design considerations

    VXLAN encapsulation is not NAT.
    Translation usually operates on the routed tenant packet at an appropriate service point.
    Tenant VRFs must align with the NAT routing context.
    Return traffic must reach the correct translation device.
    ECMP and service-node selection must respect state requirements.


Do not infer NAT support on an EVPN/VXLAN device merely because it runs IOS XE.


25. Monitoring and Troubleshooting

Common commands

Command 	Purpose
show ip nat translations 	Display translation entries
show ip nat translations verbose 	Display additional details where supported
show ip nat statistics 	Review translation statistics and resource information
show ip route 	Verify forwarding reachability
show access-lists 	Check NAT-selection matches
show route-map 	Inspect policy matching

Additional tools include supported packet captures, platform dataplane counters, NAT64 verification commands, and translation-event logging.

Troubleshooting workflow

    Identify the expected original and translated packet.
    Verify interface roles or applicable NVI configuration.
    Confirm route and VRF context.
    Check ACL and route-map selection.
    Confirm translation creation.
    Observe the packet leaving the router.
    Verify return-packet arrival.
    Confirm reverse translation and internal forwarding.
    Investigate limits, timers, ALGs, or fragments if required.


Common symptoms

Symptom 	Investigation
No translation 	Selection policy, interface roles, routing, resource limits
Translation exists but no reply 	Upstream routing, address reachability, external policy
Reply arrives but is not delivered 	Reverse mapping, VRF, internal route, asymmetric path
Application partially works 	ALG, related flows, MTU, fragments, timers
Failure after ISP switch 	Address change or missing state
Public service works only externally 	Hairpin support and return-path design

Operational warning: Detailed debugging can increase processing load. Clearing translations can terminate active connections.


