27. Summary

IOS XE NAT spans several independent dimensions:

    Static versus dynamic: How mappings are established.
    PAT: How flows share translated addresses.
    Inside-source and outside-source: Which endpoints are represented differently across NAT domains.
    ACL and policy NAT: Which traffic receives a translation.
    VRF-aware NAT: How translation integrates with routing context.
    NAT64: How IPv6 and IPv4 communicate.
    Operational capabilities: How state, application handling, resources, forwarding, and redundancy are managed.


A successful design requires more than a NAT command: translation policy, routing, return-path symmetry, platform support, and resource capacity must all agree.
